st):\n`dashboard.md` and `distributed-workloads.md` under\n`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`. Both are Konflux\nonboarded, so no onboarding chain is generated.\n\n| Strategy component | Change | Owner team | Classification | Canonical component |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Distributed Workloads | Report computation from metering data, stable report API + CSV export; final-schema alignment | Distributed Workloads | Active | Distributed Workloads |\n| Dashboard | Administrator view of consumption reports and quota comparison | RHOAI Dashboard | Active | Dashboard |\n| Documentation (implied by HLR-4) | Report semantics and export integration docs | Distributed Workloads (inferred — content is the reporting backend's domain) | Active (content authoring) | Documentation |\n\n**Non-obvious mapping logged:** the strategy does not state which component hosts\nthe report computation and stable API. It is assigned to Distributed Workloads\n(the metering/GPU domain) because the Dashboard's architecture context states the\nDashboard's only interface is the Kubernetes API — it owns no data plane and\nexposes no private component APIs — so a stable API for finance tooling cannot\nlive in the Dashboard. The Dashboard epic consumes the report surface.\n\n## Investigation Assessment\n\nThe strategy's single open question — the metering data schema finalizes only\nwhen RHAISTRAT-9990 lands — was evaluated against the Step 3 decision rule. The\nresolution is controlled by a third party (the in-flight metering strategy), not\nby any experiment this strategy can run: no Investigation epic can resolve it\nfaster. Per the external-dependency rule, it is modeled as a precondition with\ntiered delivery (Step 5.5): partial value is deliverable by building Tier 1\nagainst the current draft schema behind a schema-isolation layer, with Tier 2\nperforming final-schema alignment once RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. No Investigation\nepic is generated; the residual risk is a health warning.\n\n## Epic List\n\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Per-project GPU-hour chargeback reporting backend (Tier 1) | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Dashboard admin view for GPU chargeback reports (Tier 1) | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Align GPU-hour reporting to the final metering data schema (Tier 2) | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | Documentation for GPU chargeback report semantics and export integration | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n\n## Dependency DAG\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001[RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 reporting backend] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 Dashboard admin view]\n    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 final-schema alignment]\n    E001 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 documentation]\n    E002 --> E004\n    E003 --> E004\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 12/13 (framework/API producer → consumer serial) | The Dashboard view's code builds against the report API and data model produced by the reporting backend epic; consuming code cannot be written against an API that does not exist. |\n| E001 → E003 | Tiered delivery (Step 5.5 / Rule 18) | Tier 2 alignment reworks the schema-isolation layer the Tier 1 epic builds; it cannot start before that layer exists, and it is additionally gated by the external precondition that RHAISTRAT-9990 has landed (Risks/Notes on E003, not an AC — externally controlled timing). |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation describes the reporting capability as built. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 | Documentation covers the Dashboard admin view as built. |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 | Documentation of report semantics (what counts as a GPU-hour) must reflect the final metering schema, so docs land after the Tier 2 alignment. |\n\nE002 and E003