{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-92498bbb012188d7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018 (GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators)\n11\t\n12\t## Triage Outcome\n13\t\n14\tFull decomposition (not below-threshold, not docs-only): the strategy spans two\n15\tcomponents owned by two different teams (Dashboard — RHOAI Dashboard; Distributed\n16\tWorkloads — Distributed Workloads), and its single open question is an external\n17\tdependency with uncertain timing (the metering data schema owned by in-flight\n18\tRHAISTRAT-9990), which requires tiered delivery rather than a single epic.\n19\t\n20\t## Strategy Parsing Notes\n21\t\n22\t- **Affected Components**: Dashboard, Distributed Workloads (listed without\n23\t  per-component change detail — inferred from HLRs, see mapping below).\n24\t- **Impacted Teams table**: missing from the strategy. Teams inferred from\n25\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/` owning teams:\n26\t  Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard; Distributed Workloads → Distributed Workloads.\n27\t- **Dependencies table**: missing as a table; the frontmatter carries\n28\t  `depends-on: RHAISTRAT-9990` (GPU usage metering — in flight, separate strategy).\n29\t- **Acceptance Criteria (Given/When/Then)**: missing — epic ACs derived from HLRs.\n30\t- **Non-Functional Requirements**: missing.\n31\t- **Staff Engineer Input**: absent — no overrides to log.\n32\t\n33\tThese missing sections are recorded as health warnings (below); decomposition\n34\tproceeded with available information.\n35\t\n36\t## Step 1.5 — Staff Engineer Input\n37\t\n38\tNo Staff Engineer Input section present in the strategy; nothing to override.\n39\t\n40\t## Component Graph and Mapping\n41\t\n42\tBoth affected components are in the platform (architecture context files exist):\n43\t`dashboard.md` and `distributed-workloads.md` under\n44\t`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`. Both are Konflux\n45\tonboarded, so no onboarding chain is generated.\n46\t\n47\t| Strategy component | Change | Owner team | Classification | Canonical component |\n48\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n49\t| Distributed Workloads | Report computation from metering data, stable report API + CSV export; final-schema alignment | Distributed Workloads | Active | Distributed Workloads |\n50\t| Dashboard | Administrator view of consumption reports and quota comparison | RHOAI Dashboard | Active | Dashboard |\n51\t| Documentation (implied by HLR-4) | Report semantics and export integration docs | Distributed Workloads (inferred — content is the reporting backend's domain) | Active (content authoring) | Documentation |\n52\t\n53\t**Non-obvious mapping logged:** the strategy does not state which component hosts\n54\tthe report computation and stable API. It is assigned to Distributed Workloads\n55\t(the metering/GPU domain) because the Dashboard's architecture context states the\n56\tDashboard's only interface is the Kubernetes API — it owns no data plane and\n57\texposes no private component APIs — so a stable API for finance tooling cannot\n58\tlive in the Dashboard. The Dashboard epic consumes the report surface.\n59\t\n60\t## Investigation Assessment\n61\t\n62\tThe strategy's single open question — the metering data schema finalizes only\n63\twhen RHAISTRAT-9990 lands — was evaluated against the Step 3 decision rule. The\n64\tresolution is controlled by a third party (the in-flight metering strategy), not\n65\tby any experiment this strategy can run: no Investigation epic can resolve it\n66\tfaster. Per the external-dependency rule, it is modeled as a precondition with\n67\ttiered delivery (Step 5.5): partial value is deliverable by building Tier 1\n68\tagainst the current draft schema behind a schema-isolation layer, with Tier 2\n69\tperforming final-schema alignment once RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. No Investigation\n70\tepic is generated; the residual risk is a health warning.\n71\t\n72\t## Epic List\n73\t\n74\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n75\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n76\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Per-project GPU-hour chargeback reporting backend (Tier 1) | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n77\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Dashboard admin view for GPU chargeback reports (Tier 1) | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n78\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Align GPU-hour reporting to the final metering data schema (Tier 2) | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n79\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | Documentation for GPU chargeback report semantics and export integration | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n80\t\n81\t## Dependency DAG\n82\t\n83\t```mermaid\n84\tgraph TD\n85\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 reporting backend] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 Dashboard admin view]\n86\t    E001 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 final-schema alignment]\n87\t    E001 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 documentation]\n88\t    E002 --> E004\n89\t    E003 --> E004\n90\t```\n91\t\n92\t## DAG Justification\n93\t\n94\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n95\t|---|---|---|\n96\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 12/13 (framework/API producer → consumer serial) | The Dashboard view's code builds against the report API and data model produced by the reporting backend epic; consuming code cannot be written against an API that does not exist. |\n97\t| E001 → E003 | Tiered delivery (Step 5.5 / Rule 18) | Tier 2 alignment reworks the schema-isolation layer the Tier 1 epic builds; it cannot start before that layer exists, and it is additionally gated by the external precondition that RHAISTRAT-9990 has landed (Risks/Notes on E003, not an AC — externally controlled timing). |\n98\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation describes the reporting capability as built. |\n99\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 | Documentation covers the Dashboard admin view as built. |\n100\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 | Documentation of report semantics (what counts as a GPU-hour) must reflect the final metering schema, so docs land after the Tier 2 alignment. |\n101\t\n102\tE002 and E003 are deliberately parallel: the Tier 2 alignment changes how the\n103\treporting backend consumes the metering schema internally, not the report API\n104\tcontract the Dashboard consumes (that is the purpose of the isolation layer).\n105\t\n106\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n107\t\n108\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n109\t|---|---|---|---|\n110\t| HLR-1 — per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges | P0 | E001 (computation + retrieval), E002 (administrator viewing), E003 (final-form against delivered schema) | Full coverage: computation, viewing, and final alignment against the landed metering data. |\n111\t| HLR-2 — CSV export and stable API for finance tooling | P1 | E001 | **bundled** — CSV export and the stable API are presentation modes of the same report data surface that HLR-1's computation produces, for the same component and team. Splitting would build the same API/data structures twice and add an artificial serialization edge; the pair is one capability at the higher priority (P0). |\n112\t| HLR-3 — current-month consumption vs Kueue quota in Dashboard | P1 | E002 | **bundled** — renders in the same Dashboard admin view and over the same report data as HLR-1's viewing surface; quota is read from the platform's existing queueing configuration. Not separately schedulable without duplicating the view. |\n113\t| HLR-4 — documentation of report semantics and export integration | P2 | E004 | Docs-authoring epic; priority P1 per the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1), not inherited from HLR-4's P2. |\n114\t\n115\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n116\t\n117\t## Tiered Delivery\n118\t\n119\t- **Tier 1 (independent, start now)**: E001 (report computation, isolation layer\n120\t  against the current draft schema, stable API + CSV) and E002 (Dashboard admin\n121\t  view). Delivers administrator-visible consumption reporting without waiting for\n122\t  the metering strategy to land.\n123\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated)**: E003 — final-schema alignment, gated by the\n124\t  external precondition that RHAISTRAT-9990 has landed and its schema is final.\n125\t  If the final schema matches the draft, this epic shrinks to verification; if it\n126\t  differs, the isolation layer absorbs the change inside the reporting backend.\n127\t\n128\t## Health Warnings\n129\t\n130\t- **Missing strategy sections**: Impacted Teams table, Dependencies table,\n131\t  Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and NFRs are absent. Teams and ACs were\n132\t  inferred from HLRs and architecture context; a human should confirm team\n133\t  ownership, in particular the inferred documentation owner (Distributed\n134\t  Workloads) and the inferred placement of the report API in Distributed\n135\t  Workloads rather than the Dashboard.\n136\t- **External timing risk (the strategy's open question)**: the metering data\n137\t  schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. Mitigated by the Tier 1/Tier 2\n138\t  split and the schema-isolation layer; the residual risk is rework in E003 if\n139\t  the final schema diverges materially from the draft. E003's start date is\n140\t  externally controlled and cannot be compressed by this strategy.\n141\t- **Priority notes**: E003 is P0 because it completes HLR-1 (P0) in final form,\n142\t  even though it is Tier 2 and gated on external timing — planners should not\n143\t  read its P0 as \"start immediately\". E004 is P1 (docs-authoring exception:\n144\t  strategy priority Major → P1), which is higher than HLR-4's P2 label; this is\n145\t  the rule-mandated derivation, not a priority inversion.\n146\t- **Scope boundary enforced as AC**: the metering collection pipeline\n147\t  (RHAISTRAT-9990) is out of scope; E001 carries an explicit AC that metering is\n148\t  consumed, not re-implemented.\n149\t- No epic exceeds the ~2-week guideline; no re-split triggers required.\n150\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a1e70839a58d64d1","agent_id":"a1d62a9cc75a4342b","session_id":"1ce7f518-86cd-4dc0-977d-8d36a0eb367d","uuid":"c7fff121-eb10-44e7-8e34-f60e4eaebdaa","timestamp":"2026-10-25T17:13:24.602Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-92498bbb012188d7","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
