{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-950c3a5dc095552c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues: []\n7\terror: null\n8\t---\n9\t## Review Summary\n10\t\n11\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n12\tRecommendation: accept\n13\t\n14\tcontract validator: OK (`validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-9018` exited 0 — body\n15\tsections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority\n16\tinheritance, implementation-type parallelism, and epic-size triggers all pass).\n17\tTriage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition) with 4 epics — consistent;\n18\tmulti-epic output is expected. No conditional branch files exist, and none are\n19\trequired (no Investigation epics were generated).\n20\t\n21\t## Criterion Details\n22\t\n23\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n24\tAll four HLRs map to epics and the traceability matrix is present and accurate:\n25\tHLR-1 (P0) → E001/E002/E003 (computation, viewing, final-form alignment), HLR-2\n26\t(P1) → E001, HLR-3 (P1) → E002, HLR-4 (P2) → E004. Priority inheritance is\n27\tcorrect: E001 (P0) is the only epic blocking P0 work and it is itself P0; the\n28\tdocs-authoring epic E004 is P1 via the documented exception (strategy priority\n29\tMajor → P1), not an inheritance violation. The HLR-2 bundle into E001 and the\n30\tHLR-3 bundle into E002 are the correct shape, not priority collapse: each pair\n31\tis presentation modes of one data/view surface for the same component and team\n32\t(CSV/API on the report surface; quota comparison in the same admin view), and\n33\teach epic's HLR Traceability lists both HLRs with the bundling justified —\n34\tsplitting would build the same surface twice.\n35\t\n36\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n37\tNo cycles. Every diagram edge (E001→E002, E001→E003, E001→E004, E002→E004,\n38\tE003→E004) has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter\n39\tand vice versa; arrows run dependency → dependent per the diagram convention.\n40\tE001→E002 is a genuine API producer→consumer edge (Dashboard view builds against\n41\tthe report API). E001→E003 is a real artifact dependency (Tier 2 reworks the\n42\tisolation layer Tier 1 builds); the external RHAISTRAT-9990 timing is correctly\n43\tkept out of the DAG and out of the ACs (Risks/Notes on E003). The three edges\n44\tinto E004 are valid Rule 11 docs-last edges and are not flagged as serialization.\n45\tE002 and E003 are correctly parallel — E003 changes internal schema consumption\n46\tbehind the isolation layer, not the report API contract E002 consumes; E003's AC\n47\tphrase \"view data\" reads as the report data served to the view (an E001 output),\n48\tnot the E002 UI, so no missing edge is implied. No konflux-onboarding,\n49\trepo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism\n50\tis not implicated. Critical path 3 is appropriate (docs-last alone forces ≥3) for\n51\ta two-component strategy of this size.\n52\t\n53\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n54\tComponent/team tuples are clean: E001/E003 (Distributed Workloads / Distributed\n55\tWorkloads), E002 (Dashboard / RHOAI Dashboard), E004 (Documentation component /\n56\tDistributed Workloads team, docs-authoring). Each team's work is in its own\n57\tepic; all components are in the canonical list. No scope duplication: E001 owns\n58\tcomputation/API/CSV, E002 only consumes the report surface, E003 reworks the\n59\tisolation layer, E004 documents. No work-product mixing (code vs. content are\n60\tseparate epics). No epic exceeds the ~2-week guideline; the summary records this\n61\tand no re-split triggers are needed.\n62\t\n63\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n64\tAll four epics are Implementation and each produces a concrete artifact (report\n65\tAPI + CSV, Dashboard view, schema alignment, documentation). No Investigation\n66\tepic is warranted: the strategy's single open question (metering schema\n67\tfinalization) is controlled by a third party (RHAISTRAT-9990) and no experiment\n68\there could resolve it faster — the external-dependency/tiered-delivery treatment\n69\tis the correct call and is explicitly justified in the summary. No `gated_by` is\n70\tset anywhere, so no `gate_failure_impact` or gating-metadata consistency issues\n71\tarise.\n72\t\n73\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n74\tAll epics are Implementation type and carry `ai_signals` (none carry\n75\t`investigation_signals`) — type-appropriate. Frontmatter values match the\n76\tai-signals rationale tables exactly. Values are consistent with the strategy and\n77\tthe platform context: E001/E003 `external_dependency: -1` (in-flight\n78\tRHAISTRAT-9990 schema) and E002 `external_dependency: 0` (its only upstream is\n79\tsame-decomposition E001) are correctly differentiated; E002\n80\t`pattern_precedent: +1` / `existing_foundation: +1` are corroborated by\n81\t`dashboard.md` (single-pane console already surfacing projects and distributed\n82\tworkload status); E004 `human_process_gates: -1` matches its mandated technical\n83\treview AC. `architecture_claims: +1` is properly calibrated on all four epics —\n84\tthe rationales cite the specific architecture-context files, and the cited\n85\tclaims check out against `.context` (distributed-workloads.md: Kueue\n86\tClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota model, NVIDIA GPU Operator, Kueue metrics exposed\n87\tto Prometheus; dashboard.md: OpenShift OAuth project-scoped RBAC,\n88\tKubernetes-API-only with no private component APIs, OdhDashboardConfig feature\n89\tflags). The summary's non-obvious mapping (report API in Distributed Workloads,\n90\tnot the Dashboard) is also grounded in the dashboard.md \"no private component\n91\tAPIs\" constraint. Computed scores were not re-derived (pipeline-computed).\n92\t\n93\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n94\tEvery epic has testable ACs: E001 includes structural-check and\n95\tfixture-based-determinism tests plus an AC enforcing the out-of-scope boundary\n96\t(metering consumed, never re-implemented); E002 includes RBAC verification;\n97\tE003 includes a structural check for superseded draft-schema constructs; E004\n98\tcarries the rule-mandated technical-review sign-off AC. No rule-mandated AC is\n99\tmissing (no replacement or konflux-chain epics exist). The externally-controlled\n100\tprecondition (RHAISTRAT-9990 landing) is correctly placed in Risks/Notes and the\n101\tsummary's Health Warnings rather than misfiled as an AC — E003 states this\n102\tdistinction explicitly. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID\n103\t(verified by scan; bodies refer to \"the Tier 1 reporting work\" / \"the reporting\n104\tbackend\" in prose).\n105\t\n106\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n107\tFull strategy scope is accounted for: report computation and selectable time\n108\tranges (E001, E002), stable API and CSV export for finance tooling (E001),\n109\tcurrent-month consumption vs. Kueue quota in the Dashboard admin view (E002,\n110\tincluding administrator-only access), final-schema alignment (E003), and report\n111\tsemantics + export integration documentation (E004, covering HLR-4 and usage of\n112\tthe admin view). Both out-of-scope items (metering pipeline, currency modelling)\n113\tare explicitly excluded in epic scope and enforced by AC. Strategy-level context\n114\tis carried forward, not dropped: the open question / external timing risk is a\n115\tHealth Warning and an epic-level risk, the missing strategy sections (Impacted\n116\tTeams, Dependencies table, Given/When/Then ACs, NFRs) are recorded as Health\n117\tWarnings with the inferences (team ownership, docs owner, API placement) flagged\n118\tfor human confirmation. A docs-authoring epic exists for the documentation\n119\trequirement. No conditional branches are needed (no bounded-outcome\n120\tinvestigation).\n121\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9bd907e7fd48ef98","agent_id":"aa1b2f83bb835ae5b","session_id":"1